const hrScenarios = r"""
Beginner | Introduction | Give a 60-second introduction for a junior data role. | Start with your current focus, mention one relevant skill with evidence, and connect it to this role. Keep private details and unrelated chronology out of the pitch. | Give the interviewer a short, job-relevant starting point. | I completed a statistics degree and built a SQL ticket dashboard that flagged overdue cases. I'd like to apply that mix of analysis and communication in your support analytics team.
Beginner | Strengths | Which strength would your teammates point to, and what backs it up? | Choose a strength relevant to the job and describe an observable action and result. Credit others where the result was shared. | Give an example somebody else could verify. | My teammates relied on my debugging notes. I documented reproduction steps that helped two teammates fix the import error.
Beginner | Weaknesses | Describe a real weakness and how you are working on it. | Choose a genuine development area, explain when it affects you and describe a specific improvement habit. Include evidence of progress where available. | Explain what you are doing differently now. | I used to wait too long to ask for help. I now write down what I've tried and ask after 30 minutes on unfamiliar blockers.
Beginner | Career goals | Where would you like to grow in the next two years? | Name skills and responsibilities you want to earn, connect them to this role, and give a plausible milestone. Avoid promising an exact promotion date. | Explain what you want to learn and contribute. | I want to own recurring reports end to end, then lead a small improvement project after demonstrating reliable delivery.
Beginner | Project ownership | What part of your group project did you personally own? | Define the team's objective, identify your responsibilities and show your result. Distinguish your contributions from work by others. | Make your individual work visible. | My team built the app. I owned CSV validation and wrote the tests for missing IDs.
Beginner | Learning | How did you teach yourself a tool for your first project? | Describe the task, your learning resources, a small exercise and how you checked understanding by applying it. | Show how you turn new material into a result. | I learned SQL joins from documentation, built two toy tables, then validated a real join against a manual count.
Beginner | Teamwork | How did you contribute when a teammate was behind? | State how you checked the blocker, agreed on priorities and shared work without obscuring accountability. Include the outcome. | Offer targeted help and keep ownership clear. | We split test cases and agreed that my teammate would still review the final changes.
Beginner | Communication | Explain a project to someone who knows nothing about the tools you used. | State who had the problem, what your work changed and what evidence supports the result. Use familiar words and check understanding. | Explain the outcome before discussing tools. | We made overdue requests visible each morning, giving staff one list to work through.
Beginner | Planning | How did you organize your work when two assignments were due the same day? | Compare deadlines and effort, split each into milestones and protect time for verification. Communicate early if a dependency changes. | Plan around deadlines, effort and blockers. | I finished the shared slide draft first so others could review it while I completed the individual assignment.
Beginner | Reliability | What would you do if you could not attend a scheduled interview? | Notify the recruiter promptly, state the conflict briefly and respectfully, and offer concrete rescheduling windows. Confirm any changed appointment. | Give early notice and make rescheduling easy. | I have a scheduling conflict on Tuesday. Could we move to Wednesday at 11 a.m. or Thursday at 2 p.m.?
Beginner | Reflection | What would you change if you could redo your first project? | Name a specific lesson and a remedy that would improve the result. Explain what evidence led you to that conclusion. | Use hindsight to explain a better decision. | I'd define the data validation rules earlier because format conflicts delayed the final report.
Beginner | Interview preparation | What question would you ask a hiring manager at the end of an interview? | Ask about the team's work, success criteria or onboarding. Choose something relevant that the conversation has left unanswered. | Ask for information that helps you understand the job. | What would a successful first three months look like for someone in this role?
Beginner | Transferable skills | How would you explain a change from a nontechnical degree to a data role? | Describe the experience that sparked the change, the skills carried over and evidence of new technical practice. Connect both to the role. | Show continuity and preparation. | Economics taught me to assess evidence. Building a reproducible Python analysis helped me apply that interest.
Beginner | Quality | How do you check your work before sending it to a teammate? | Verify requirements, test representative and edge cases, and summarize what changed. Call out anything the reviewer should still confirm. | Catch predictable errors before handing over work. | I match dashboard totals against the source and state the filters used.
Beginner | Work habits | How do you keep track of tasks assigned in different meetings? | Capture tasks in one place with an owner and due date, confirm vague requests, and review the list regularly. | Turn meeting notes into explicit commitments. | Add review query by Thursday to the team board and link the request.
Intermediate | Stakeholder communication | How would you respond when a stakeholder asks for an unclear dashboard? | Ask what decision they need to make, who uses it and which metrics indicate success. Sketch a small prototype and confirm definitions before committing. | Clarify the decision before building the report. | A request for a sales dashboard becomes a weekly view of unfulfilled orders by region.
Intermediate | Accountability | Describe a deadline you missed and how you handled it. | State the missed commitment, how you communicated it, what changed in scope or schedule and how you prevented recurrence. Own decisions within your control. | Explain the recovery and prevention. | I told the lead when an upstream delay surfaced, shipped a reduced view first and tracked source readiness next cycle.
Intermediate | Handovers | How would you hand over an analysis to someone in a different time zone? | Provide purpose, sources, definitions, run instructions, known risks and named owners. Verify the successor can reproduce one concrete result asynchronously. | Make the next person able to continue without a meeting. | Include the query, schedule, output example and how to investigate a failed refresh.
Intermediate | Leadership | Describe influencing a decision when you had no authority. | Explain the shared goal, how you listened, what evidence or pilot you offered and how stakeholders chose. State the impact without overstating your role. | Use evidence and trust to move the decision. | Surveyed users, demonstrated a simpler workflow and invited the owner to choose after a pilot.
Intermediate | Adaptability | What do you do when project requirements change late? | Confirm the new outcome, identify affected work and commitments, and offer clear scope and schedule options. Document the final agreement. | Update the plan with the people affected. | Deliver only the new priority region this week and agree on a later date for the remaining regions.
Intermediate | Customer service | How would you respond to a client who received an incorrect report? | Acknowledge the issue, verify impact and advise whether to pause use. Set a time for the next update, share the corrected version, and explain prevention after confirming the cause. | Protect the client's decisions and give clear updates. | Ask the client to hold the affected deck while totals are reconciled and promise a status update by noon.
Intermediate | Mentoring | How would you help a new teammate who keeps making the same mistake? | Observe the workflow, ask how they understand it, and demonstrate the correction. Let them try, then add a supporting checklist or tool and follow up. | Teach the cause and make the correct action easier. | Pair on one safe release, ask them to run the next and add a preflight check.
Intermediate | Judgement | How would you decide when a report is good enough to send? | Agree on required accuracy, completeness and timing. Check high-impact assumptions, communicate open limitations and defer cosmetic work when it does not affect decisions. | Focus verification on what could change the decision. | Publish the validated regional totals with a note that subregion data will follow.
Intermediate | Conflict repair | Tell me how you rebuilt trust after a disagreement became personal. | Acknowledge specific behavior, give the other person room to describe impact, and agree on changes for future work. Follow through and separate facts from interpretation. | Repair the relationship through observable change. | I acknowledged interrupting, asked how they wanted to revisit the decision and used a structured turn-taking format at the next meeting.
Intermediate | Career transition | Why did you leave a previous role? | Answer accurately and briefly. Explain relevant changes, what you learned and what you seek next while protecting confidential details. | Explain the transition professionally. | My contract ended after the reporting migration. I'm now looking for a role where I can own ongoing analytics.
Intermediate | Boundaries | How would you respond when asked to perform regular work outside agreed hours? | Clarify frequency, urgency and expectations, discuss coverage or schedule adjustments, and agree on a sustainable plan with your manager. | Clarify coverage and capacity directly. | Offer a documented on-call rotation for true emergencies while moving routine requests to daytime.
Intermediate | Remote work | How do you make blockers visible while working remotely? | Give timely written updates with impact, what you tried, what you need and when a decision is necessary. Escalate through the agreed channel if the blocker threatens delivery. | Say clearly what is stuck and who can help. | Post that access is blocking validation, link the ticket and ask a named owner to approve it today.
Intermediate | Interview logistics | How would you answer a question about salary expectations before you know the role scope? | Ask about scope and the budgeted range, then share expectations if you can anchor them to the role and total compensation. Be clear about any meaningful constraints. | Exchange enough context for a useful compensation discussion. | Could you share the role's level and budgeted range? I'd like to understand the overall package and responsibilities.
Intermediate | Documentation | What would you do if an important process existed only in one person's head? | Identify critical steps and failure modes, shadow an execution, write a runbook and have a second person validate it. Assign ownership for updates. | Capture the process and test the instructions. | A teammate follows the payroll report runbook independently on sample data.
Advanced | Ethics | What do you do if a manager asks you to omit a metric that undermines a presentation? | Clarify the intended audience and reason, explain how omission could mislead, and propose contextualizing the metric. Use appropriate escalation channels for material misrepresentation. | Present the evidence fairly and preserve accountability. | Show both conversion and refunds, with a short explanation of why conversions rose while net value fell.
Advanced | Incident response | What would you communicate after discovering an incident that affects customer-facing estimates? | Notify the right incident owner, describe known scope and uncertainty, and establish cadence. Separate mitigation, customer communication and follow-up actions with owners. | Share verified facts early and update as evidence changes. | Post that estimates from one region are stale, disable the view and give the next status time while checking other regions.
Advanced | Decision-making | How would you challenge an executive's proposal when your evidence is incomplete? | Clarify the objective, explain what is known and unknown, and quantify downside where feasible. Propose a decision deadline and a reversible test whenever practical. | Make uncertainty visible and propose a way to reduce it. | Flag that the forecast excludes recent churn and propose a regional pilot with an exit threshold.
Advanced | Cross-functional delivery | How do you address a dependency team that repeatedly misses agreed dates? | Investigate incentives and constraints, establish a jointly owned agreement and observable milestones, and escalate delivery risk with options. Avoid attributing intent without evidence. | Resolve the coordination problem and clarify ownership. | Agree on weekly schema readiness checks and prepare a fallback extract with the delivery lead.
Advanced | Process improvements | How would you prove that a new review process helps instead of adding overhead? | Define cycle time, defect rate and workload before the pilot, compare with a realistic baseline, and watch for case mix changes. Gather reviewer feedback and set a decision point. | Measure benefits and costs with the same criteria. | Pilot review on one report stream and track rework minutes and late defects for a month.
Advanced | Diversity and inclusion | What would you do if a qualified teammate is routinely interrupted in meetings? | Intervene constructively, restore the floor and make meeting practices equitable. Check privately how the teammate wants support and raise persistent behavior through appropriate channels. | Improve the discussion so people can finish their contributions. | Say that you'd like to hear the rest of their point before moving to questions.
Advanced | Delegation | How would you delegate a critical deliverable to someone new to the task? | Clarify scope, standards, decision authority and check-in points. Provide context and resources, review early milestones and retain accountability for the overall outcome. | Give someone genuine ownership with clear support. | Have a new analyst draft the data contract and check the grain with them before they build.
Advanced | Confidentiality | How do you discuss a project in interviews when details are confidential? | Explain the problem class, your decisions and allowed outcomes in general terms. Use invented numbers only if clearly identified as illustrative, and respect relevant agreements. | Demonstrate your skills without sharing protected information. | Describe reconciling inconsistent ledgers, omitting the client's identity and true transaction volumes.
Advanced | Strategy | How would you decide whether to sunset an internal report with a few vocal users? | Identify usage, critical decisions, maintenance cost and alternatives. Speak to users, design a migration or exception path, and set success criteria and a notice period. | Understand the report's real work before retiring it. | Find that two users rely on export for month-end, migrate their workflow, then retire after one verified cycle.
Advanced | Change management | How would you introduce a new workflow when the team is already overloaded? | Identify the most costly friction, involve affected people, pilot with bounded effort and replace existing work where feasible. Measure adoption and impact before broad rollout. | Start small and make room for the change. | Replace one manual status meeting with an asynchronous trial for a month.
Advanced | Capacity planning | What would you do when a team commits to more work than it can deliver? | Quantify capacity and dependencies, publish consequences, and negotiate scope, staffing or dates with the sponsor. Track commitments and revisit assumptions as work progresses. | Match promises to realistic capacity. | Show that three integrations overlap the same specialist and agree on one now and two later.
Advanced | Decision records | What would you document about a contentious decision after the team has committed? | Record the decision, evidence, alternatives, trade-offs, owner and conditions for reconsideration. Give dissent a neutral account while making execution responsibilities clear. | Preserve why the decision was made and when to revisit it. | Record the decision to build one regional pilot, the capacity trade-off and the threshold for expanding.
Advanced | Project recovery | How would you take over a project with uncertain status and an imminent milestone? | Establish a verified inventory of deliverables, dependencies and risks, assess critical path, align on what must ship and communicate a revised plan. | Confirm reality before making delivery promises. | Test whether the import runs on production-shaped data, then cut a cosmetic export to protect the verified path.
Advanced | Management feedback | How do you tell a manager their decision is creating avoidable churn? | Use specific examples and their business cost, ask about underlying constraints, and propose a practical experiment. Choose a respectful and appropriate forum. | Discuss the effect of the decision with evidence. | Share the hours spent on repeated last-minute changes and propose a weekly decision cutoff for routine requests.
Advanced | Organizational change | What would you do if a reorganization leaves two teams thinking the other owns a critical service? | Identify immediate operational coverage, document responsibilities and gaps, get an accountable decision-maker to assign ownership, and verify handover. | Assign a temporary owner now and a permanent one explicitly. | Keep the current on-call rotation active until the new team accepts alerts, permissions and runbooks.
Advanced | Vendor management | How would you respond when a vendor offers a quick fix that bypasses formal review? | Clarify the failure and urgency, use the organization's approved emergency process if needed, and check evidence, rollback and responsibility before applying the fix. | Move quickly within the incident process. | Schedule an emergency change with two reviewers and a verified rollback step.
Advanced | Strategic communication | How would you explain a failed initiative to senior stakeholders? | Summarize the goal, observed outcomes, reasons supported by evidence, costs and recommended next action. Distinguish what is learned from what still needs validation. | Explain the outcome and the next decision clearly. | The pilot reduced cycle time but increased corrections. Recommend retiring it and testing the validation idea separately.
Beginner | Availability | How would you explain your earliest realistic start date? | State the earliest date you can commit to, mention relevant availability constraints briefly and confirm the employer's target. | Give a date you can honor. | I can start on June 15 after my final exams. Does that fit the onboarding schedule?
Beginner | Applications | What information belongs in a follow-up after submitting a portfolio? | Identify the role and submission date, link the relevant work and ask one clear question or offer to provide more context. | Make it easy for the recruiter to locate and assess your work. | I submitted my analyst application Tuesday. The second portfolio link shows my SQL project and validation notes.
Intermediate | Career breaks | How would you explain a gap on your résumé? | Give an accurate, brief explanation at the level of detail you are comfortable sharing. State your readiness and highlight relevant work or practice where applicable. | Explain the timeline and shift back to job readiness. | I took time away for family commitments. I am available now and recently refreshed my reporting skills through a project.
Intermediate | Scope negotiation | How do you turn down a request that belongs to another team? | Clarify the need and urgency, route it to the accountable owner with context and confirm who will follow up. Help with dependencies you own. | Make the handoff clear so the request still moves. | Introduce the finance data owner and share the exact reconciliation question and due date.
Intermediate | Knowledge sharing | How would you make a retrospective produce useful action? | Gather concrete examples, choose one or two process changes with owners and review dates, and agree on how to tell whether they helped. | End with small commitments that the team can check. | Assign the release checklist change to one owner and compare missed steps after two releases.
Advanced | Hiring | How would you compare interview candidates with different types of experience? | Use common job-related criteria, ask for comparable evidence, score independently before discussing and document how evidence supports each rating. | Compare demonstrated skills against the role requirements. | Evaluate debugging via a common exercise whether a candidate learned it at work or through an open-source project.

""";
